# Understanding LED States During Ignition Cycle vs. Runtime

When a driver notices dashboard indicators behaving counter-intuitively, it often stems from a misunderstanding of the vehicle's power sequencing rather than an immediate mechanical failure. In your specific case involving a 2005 Chrysler Sebring, the behavior you observed—where the Check Engine Light (CEL) and Oil Pressure Warning light remain illuminated while the key is in the "accessory" or "run" position but before cranking—is actually standard operation for modern electronic control units (ECUs).

The core issue lies in distinguishing between system initialization and active monitoring. When you turn the ignition to the accessory mode, the ECU powers up its internal processors and memory but does not engage the fuel injectors or spark plugs yet. During this window, the engine is mechanically stationary, meaning oil pressure cannot exist. Consequently, the oil pressure sensor circuit reads zero volts (or an open circuit), which the dashboard interprets as a fault condition. The system triggers the warning light to alert the driver that critical data is unavailable until the pump engages.

Similarly, the Check Engine Light serves two distinct purposes depending on the ignition state. During the start-up sequence, it functions as a "bulb check" or self-test indicator. The ECU grounds the lamp briefly to verify that the bulb and wiring harness are intact. If this light fails to illuminate when the key is turned but does not turn off within 20 seconds of starting the engine, then there is an active fault code stored in memory. However, if it stays on while you have the car in accessory mode and turns off once the engine starts, the bulb check has completed successfully.

From a software engineering perspective, this mirrors the difference between application initialization and runtime execution. Consider how many web frameworks handle dependency injection or service loading before the main request loop begins:

```php
// Pseudo-code analogy for ECU startup sequence
public function boot() {
    $this->enableSelfTestLamps(); // Turns on CEL momentarily to verify hardware

    if (!$this->engineCranked) {
        // Engine is off; cannot measure oil pressure yet
        $this->triggerWarning('OilPressureSensorReadingNull'); 
        return;
    }

    // Engine running; sensors active
    while ($this->isRunning()) {
        $pressure = $this->oilPressureSensor->read();
        if ($pressure < THRESHOLD) {
            $this->triggerCriticalWarning('LowOilPressure');
        }

        if (!$this->hasActiveFaults()) {
            $this->clearLamps(); // Turns off CEL if no codes exist
        }
    }
}
```

In your scenario, the ECU is executing the `boot()` phase. It knows the engine isn't running yet, so it displays warnings for conditions that logically cannot be evaluated (like oil pressure) or performs hardware checks. Once you crank the starter motor, the alternator charges the battery, the water pump and oil pump spin up, and real-time sensor data becomes available. At this point, the ECU switches to the runtime loop where it actively clears transient warnings and monitors live data streams.

There is a nuance regarding your observation of other diagnostic lights remaining off before starting. Most modern vehicles suppress non-critical alerts during the accessory phase to reduce driver distraction, focusing only on safety-critical or hardware verification tasks like the CEL bulb check. If you were seeing multiple warning lights flash erratically in accessory mode, that would indicate a more serious communication bus issue between the ECU and other modules like the instrument cluster or ABS controller.

Since your lights extinguish immediately upon engine startup, the likelihood of an active fault is low, provided your oil level is indeed within the safe range on the dipstick. The "slightly down" observation warrants attention, but the lighting behavior itself confirms that the dashboard logic and sensors are responding correctly to the ignition cycle states.
